  "terminology_notes": {
    "_description": "Important terminology differences and gotchas",
    "notes": [
      "NIST uses 'information owner' — never 'data owner'. The ER crosswalks and ISO use 'data owner' informally.",
      "The acronyms ISSO and ISSM never appear in NIST 800-53 catalog text — only the full titles 'information system security officer' and 'information system security manager' are used.",
      "The exact phrase 'security control assessor' does not appear in the catalog — NIST uses 'control assessor' or simply 'assessor'.",
      "HIPAA uses 'workforce member' which is broader than 'employee' — includes volunteers and trainees.",
      "SOC 2 'service organization' vs ISO 27001 'organization' vs HIPAA 'covered entity' — all refer to the entity being assessed/regulated but with different legal definitions.",
      "'Covered entity' is fundamental to HIPAA but never explicitly named in the ER crosswalk data — it's treated as implicit context.",
      "GDPR 'controller' and SOC 2 Privacy 'data controller' are the same concept but arise from different legal frameworks.",
      "FedRAMP 'CSP' (Cloud Service Provider) is distinct from NIST 800-63B 'CSP' (Credential Service Provider) — same acronym, different meaning.",
      "HITRUST 'inheritance' in SRM refers to control inheritance (shared responsibility), not organizational hierarchy.",
      "The dbz source_manifest.json 'role' field is a source-file-type classifier (e.g., 'nist_catalog', 'overlap_oracle'), NOT an organizational role."
    ]
  }
